<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Abbas ibn Firnas</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 810–<a href="/years/887">887</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/9th-century">9th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Abbas ibn Firnas was an Andalusi polymath of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berbers">Berber</a> descent at the court of the Umayyad <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emir">emirs</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cordoba">Córdoba</a> in the ninth century. A poet, astronomer and inventor, he is credited with a method of cutting rock crystal and with a planetarium-like device, and according to a later account by al-Maqqari he attempted to fly with a feathered apparatus, injuring himself on landing.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/abbas-ibn-firnas.jpg" alt="Abbas ibn Firnas" width="320" height="372" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Eulogia Merle · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Armen_Firman_Abb%C3%A1s_Ibn_Firn%C3%A1s_(MUNCYT,_Eulogia_Merle).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Abū al-Qāsim ʿAbbās ibn Firnās ibn Wardās al-Tākurunnī, who lived from about 809 or 810 to 887, was an Andalusi polymath: an inventor, astronomer, physician, chemist, engineer, musician and poet. Scholarly consensus holds that he was born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ronda">Ronda</a>, in the province of Takurunna, lived in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cordoba">Córdoba</a> and was an Umayyad mawlā, or client, of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berbers">Berber</a> descent. His lineage may have been linked to the early Muslim conquests of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iberia">Iberian Peninsula</a>. Later accounts that stress Arabic contributions to science may describe him as a muladí.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Works and ideas</h2><p>Ibn Firnas devised a way of making colourless glass, made glass planispheres and corrective lenses known as reading stones, and built an apparatus with a chain of objects that simulated the motions of the planets and stars. He developed a process for cutting rock crystal, which allowed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/al-andalus">al-Andalus</a> to stop exporting quartz to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> to be cut. He introduced the Sindhind to al-Andalus, with an influence on astronomy in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>, and designed a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/water-clock">water clock</a>, the al-Maqata, and a prototype of a metronome.</p><p>He is also reported to have experimented with unpowered flight. The earliest known account is in a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manuscript">manuscript</a> of the Cordoban court chronicle al-Muqtabis II, attributed to Ibn Hayyan, which says he covered himself in feathers fastened to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silk">silk</a>, fitted two wings, rose into the air from near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rusafa">Rusafa</a>, circled and landed far away, but injured his tailbone because he had not accounted for how birds land on their tails. The Algerian historian Ahmad al-Maqqari, writing some seven centuries later, gave a fuller version without naming his sources. Anderson reads the earlier account as describing a fixed-wing glide, while Richard Hallion thinks a medieval <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/parachute">parachute</a> drop with a cloak more likely.</p><p>A story that he watched a man called Armen Firman jump from a tower about twenty years earlier rests on limited secondary sources, and Armen Firman may be a Latinized form of his own name.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Ibn Firnas died in 887. In 1973 a statue of him by Badri al-Samarrai was installed at Baghdad International Airport, and in 1976 the International Astronomical Union named a lunar crater after him. The Abbas ibn Firnás Bridge in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cordoba">Córdoba</a>, completed in 2011, bears his name.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Abbas ibn Firnas” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The musician Ziryab, who arrived from Iraq in 822, is credited by later tradition with introducing new fashions in music, dress, cuisine and manners at Córdoba, and the polymath <b>Abbas ibn Firnas</b> experimented with glass-making and, reportedly, with flight.</blockquote><a href="/empire/emirate-of-cordoba">Emirate of Córdoba · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ronda">Ronda</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/spain">Spain</a>. Died in Córdoba, in what is now <a href="/countries/spain">Spain</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=36.74,-5.17&amp;year=860" aria-label="Ronda on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of Abbas ibn Firnas"><image href="/maps/globes/g000n30.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4646 4417h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4646 4417h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=36.74,-5.17&amp;year=860">What was here in 860 CE? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
